steps.text:
The error means that `props.items` is **undefined** (or `null`) when the component tries to call `.map()` on it. In JavaScript, only arrays (or array‑like objects) have a `map` method, so you need to make sure the component always receives an array.

Below are the most common ways to fix this, followed by a small, self‑contained example.

---

## 1. Give the parent component a proper value

The simplest fix is to guarantee that the component’s caller always passes an array, even when there are no items.

```jsx
// Parent component
function Parent() {
  const data = fetchData();               // could be [] or an array
  // Make sure it’s always an array
  const items = Array.isArray(data) ? data : [];

  return <ItemList items={items} />;
}
```

---

## 2. Provide a default prop (or default parameter) inside the component

If you can’t control the caller, give the component a fallback value.

### a. Using default props (class or function component)

```jsx
// For a function component
function ItemList({ items = [] }) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map(item => (
        <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

### b. Using `defaultProps` (works for both class and function components)

```jsx
function ItemList({ items }) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map(item => (
        <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}

// Set a default so `items` is never undefined
ItemList.defaultProps = {
  items: [],
};
```

> **Note:** With modern React (especially when using TypeScript or ES6 default parameters), the first pattern (`{ items = [] }`) is usually preferred.

---

## 3. Guard the rendering with a conditional check

If you want to show something else when there are no items, add an explicit guard.

```jsx
function ItemList({ items }) {
  if (!Array.isArray(items) || items.length === 0) {
    return <p>No items to display.</p>;
  }

  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map(item => (
        <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

---

## 4. Use optional chaining (ES2020+)

If you only need to avoid the crash and don’t mind rendering nothing when `items` is missing:

```jsx
function ItemList({ items }) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {items?.map(item => (
        <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}
```

`items?.map` evaluates to `undefined` when `items` is `null` or `undefined`, so React renders nothing instead of throwing.

---

## 5. Type‑checking / PropTypes (optional but helpful)

Adding runtime checks can catch missing props early in development.

```jsx
import PropTypes from 'prop-types';

function ItemList({ items }) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map(item => (
        <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}

ItemList.propTypes = {
  // `isRequired` will warn if the prop is missing
  items: PropTypes.array.isRequired,
};
```

If you’re using TypeScript, declare the prop type:

```tsx
type Item = { id: string; name: string };
type Props = { items: Item[] };

const ItemList: React.FC<Props> = ({ items }) => (
  <ul>
    {items.map(item => (
      <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>
    ))}
  </ul>
);
```

---

## Quick Checklist

| ✅ | What to verify |
|---|---|
| **Parent supplies an array** | Ensure the data source always returns `[]` instead of `null/undefined`. |
| **Component has a fallback** | Use default parameters (`items = []`) or `defaultProps`. |
| **Guard against bad values** | `Array.isArray(items)` before calling `.map`. |
| **Optional chaining** | `items?.map(...)` if you only need to avoid a crash. |
| **Prop validation** | Add `PropTypes` or TypeScript typings. |

---

### Minimal Example (copy‑paste ready)

```jsx
import React from 'react';
import PropTypes from 'prop-types';

function ItemList({ items = [] }) {
  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map(item => (
        <li key={item.id}>{item.name}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  );
}

// Optional: runtime prop validation
ItemList.propTypes = {
  items: PropTypes.array,
};

export default ItemList;
```

Now, regardless of whether the parent passes `items`, an empty array, or forgets the prop entirely, the component won’t throw `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'map')`.

---

### TL;DR

1. **Make sure `items` is always an array** – default to `[]`.
2. **Add a guard** (`items?.map` or `if (!Array.isArray(items)) …`).
3. **Use PropTypes/TypeScript** to catch missing props early.

Implement one (or a combination) of the above and the error will disappear. If you still see the issue, double‑check the data flow that supplies `props.items` and verify it isn’t being overwritten or set to `undefined` after the initial render.
